1
00:00:00,347 --> 00:00:02,017
Right now, in the middle of the Pacific

2
00:00:02,057 --> 00:00:04,357
Ocean, a patch of water is heating up to

3
00:00:04,397 --> 00:00:06,167
levels never recorded before.

4
00:00:06,317 --> 00:00:07,837
It's thousands of kilometres from any

5
00:00:07,877 --> 00:00:08,737
major city.

6
00:00:08,777 --> 00:00:10,217
And yet, over the next few months,

7
00:00:10,367 --> 00:00:11,917
it will help decide whether Australia

8
00:00:11,957 --> 00:00:14,027
burns, whether Peru floods,

9
00:00:14,177 --> 00:00:16,067
and how much you pay for your morning coffee.

10
00:00:16,172 --> 00:00:17,717
Its name is El Niño.

11
00:00:17,867 --> 00:00:19,297
And this one could be one of the strongest

12
00:00:19,337 --> 00:00:20,237
ever measured.

13
00:00:21,050 --> 00:00:22,060
To understand El Niño,

14
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:23,330
start with a normal year.

15
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:25,390
Along the equator, steady winds called the

16
00:00:25,430 --> 00:00:27,590
trade winds blow from east to west,

17
00:00:27,740 --> 00:00:29,450
from South America toward Asia.

18
00:00:29,510 --> 00:00:31,270
They push warm surface water across the

19
00:00:31,310 --> 00:00:33,460
Pacific, so it piles up near Indonesia and

20
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:34,270
Australia.

21
00:00:34,310 --> 00:00:35,830
There, the sea is actually about half a

22
00:00:35,870 --> 00:00:37,280
metre higher than off Peru.

23
00:00:37,430 --> 00:00:38,810
Meanwhile, off South America,

24
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:41,180
cold water rises from the deep to replace it.

25
00:00:41,330 --> 00:00:43,520
Warm in the west, cold in the east.

26
00:00:43,670 --> 00:00:45,200
That's the Pacific's default setting.

27
00:00:46,150 --> 00:00:48,100
Now, every few years, something breaks.

28
00:00:48,250 --> 00:00:49,440
The trade winds weaken.

29
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:50,740
Sometimes they even reverse.

30
00:00:50,890 --> 00:00:52,530
And all that warm water piled up in the

31
00:00:52,570 --> 00:00:54,720
west starts sloshing back east,

32
00:00:54,760 --> 00:00:55,990
like water in a bathtub.

33
00:00:56,140 --> 00:00:58,330
The cold water off Peru stops rising.

34
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:00,010
The eastern Pacific heats up.

35
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:02,220
This matters because warm ocean means more

36
00:01:02,260 --> 00:01:03,863
evaporation, more clouds,

37
00:01:03,903 --> 00:01:04,650
more rain.

38
00:01:04,690 --> 00:01:06,060
So when the warm water moves,

39
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:07,360
the rain moves with it.

40
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:09,060
The storms that normally soak Indonesia

41
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:11,040
drift toward the middle of the Pacific and

42
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:11,790
South America.

43
00:01:11,830 --> 00:01:13,556
And because the atmosphere is connected,

44
00:01:13,706 --> 00:01:15,446
this shift ripples across the planet,

45
00:01:15,596 --> 00:01:17,776
bending jet streams and shifting monsoons

46
00:01:17,816 --> 00:01:19,256
thousands of kilometres away.

47
00:01:19,406 --> 00:01:21,476
Fishermen in Peru noticed it centuries ago:

48
00:01:21,626 --> 00:01:23,546
warm water arriving around Christmas.

49
00:01:23,696 --> 00:01:24,806
They called it El Niño,

50
00:01:24,956 --> 00:01:26,816
the child, after the baby Jesus.

51
00:01:27,756 --> 00:01:29,576
Scientists track El Niño by watching one

52
00:01:29,616 --> 00:01:31,326
box of ocean in the central Pacific,

53
00:01:31,386 --> 00:01:33,026
called Niño 3.4.

54
00:01:33,066 --> 00:01:34,346
When it stays more than half a degree

55
00:01:34,386 --> 00:01:35,946
warmer than usual for several months,

56
00:01:36,096 --> 00:01:37,266
El Niño is declared.

57
00:01:37,416 --> 00:01:39,216
This year, it's not half a degree.

58
00:01:39,366 --> 00:01:40,706
The World Meteorological Organization

59
00:01:40,746 --> 00:01:42,266
expects that box to average around

60
00:01:42,306 --> 00:01:44,666
3.7 degrees warmer than normal from

61
00:01:44,706 --> 00:01:47,076
October to December, with a peak around December.

62
00:01:47,146 --> 00:01:49,346
It says El Niño is firmly established and

63
00:01:49,386 --> 00:01:50,556
strengthening rapidly,

64
00:01:50,676 --> 00:01:52,736
and gives it a near 100% chance of lasting

65
00:01:52,776 --> 00:01:54,456
through February 2027.

66
00:01:54,606 --> 00:01:56,226
Put it next to the giants of the past:

67
00:01:56,376 --> 00:01:59,736
1982, 1997, 2015.

68
00:01:59,886 --> 00:02:01,146
This one is in the same league,

69
00:02:01,296 --> 00:02:02,196
or beyond.

70
00:02:02,926 --> 00:02:04,276
So what happens on the ground?

71
00:02:04,426 --> 00:02:06,196
El Niño doesn't hit everyone the same way.

72
00:02:06,346 --> 00:02:07,576
It reshuffles the weather.

73
00:02:07,726 --> 00:02:09,276
In Indonesia, the Philippines and

74
00:02:09,316 --> 00:02:11,356
Australia, rain tends to disappear.

75
00:02:11,506 --> 00:02:13,416
Drought brings crop losses and raises the

76
00:02:13,456 --> 00:02:14,986
risk of massive wildfires.

77
00:02:15,136 --> 00:02:17,266
Southern Africa often faces failed harvests.

78
00:02:17,416 --> 00:02:18,856
India's monsoon can weaken.

79
00:02:19,006 --> 00:02:20,896
In northern Brazil, the Amazon can dry out.

80
00:02:21,046 --> 00:02:23,056
Meanwhile, other places get too much water.

81
00:02:23,206 --> 00:02:24,576
The coasts of Peru and Ecuador can see

82
00:02:24,616 --> 00:02:26,612
torrential rain and deadly floods in

83
00:02:26,652 --> 00:02:27,786
normally dry deserts.

84
00:02:27,826 --> 00:02:29,586
Southern Brazil, Uruguay and northern

85
00:02:29,626 --> 00:02:31,716
Argentina, the southern United States,

86
00:02:31,756 --> 00:02:32,866
parts of East Africa:

87
00:02:33,016 --> 00:02:33,976
all tend to get wetter.

88
00:02:34,126 --> 00:02:35,296
And almost everywhere,

89
00:02:35,356 --> 00:02:36,976
the dice are loaded toward heat.

90
00:02:37,126 --> 00:02:38,496
The World Meteorological Organization

91
00:02:38,536 --> 00:02:40,026
forecasts above-average temperatures

92
00:02:40,066 --> 00:02:41,466
across large parts of the globe through

93
00:02:41,506 --> 00:02:42,336
the end of the year.

94
00:02:42,376 --> 00:02:44,326
Same ocean, opposite disasters.

95
00:02:45,259 --> 00:02:46,479
You don't have to live in these places to

96
00:02:46,519 --> 00:02:47,139
feel it.

97
00:02:47,179 --> 00:02:48,739
El Niño is an economic event.

98
00:02:49,039 --> 00:02:50,919
Cocoa, coffee, palm oil,

99
00:02:50,959 --> 00:02:52,899
rice and sugar are grown in regions it

100
00:02:52,939 --> 00:02:55,119
hits hardest, so droughts there show up as

101
00:02:55,159 --> 00:02:56,899
higher prices in supermarkets everywhere.

102
00:02:57,049 --> 00:02:58,839
Off Peru, the warm water drives away the

103
00:02:58,879 --> 00:03:00,159
anchovies that feed one of the world's

104
00:03:00,199 --> 00:03:02,349
biggest fisheries, which affects fishmeal

105
00:03:02,389 --> 00:03:03,739
and fish farming worldwide.

106
00:03:03,934 --> 00:03:05,769
Past strong El Niños have cost the global

107
00:03:05,809 --> 00:03:07,419
economy trillions of dollars over the

108
00:03:07,459 --> 00:03:08,359
following years.

109
00:03:09,062 --> 00:03:09,962
There's one more twist.

110
00:03:10,112 --> 00:03:12,182
El Niño doesn't add new heat to the planet.

111
00:03:12,332 --> 00:03:14,432
But it moves heat from the ocean into the air.

112
00:03:14,582 --> 00:03:15,952
And this time, it arrives on top of

113
00:03:15,992 --> 00:03:17,552
decades of global warming.

114
00:03:17,702 --> 00:03:19,612
That's why the years following strong El

115
00:03:19,652 --> 00:03:22,112
Niños tend to break global temperature records.

116
00:03:22,262 --> 00:03:23,552
2016 did.

117
00:03:23,672 --> 00:03:24,902
2024 did.

118
00:03:25,052 --> 00:03:27,932
Many scientists expect 2027 could be next.

119
00:03:28,696 --> 00:03:30,046
El Niño is natural.

120
00:03:30,145 --> 00:03:32,026
It's been happening for thousands of years.

121
00:03:32,176 --> 00:03:34,276
What's new is the world it's landing in.

122
00:03:34,426 --> 00:03:35,286
Over the next few months,

123
00:03:35,326 --> 00:03:37,176
a patch of ocean most people will never

124
00:03:37,216 --> 00:03:38,736
see will shape their weather,

125
00:03:38,776 --> 00:03:40,606
their food, and their year.

126
00:03:40,756 --> 00:03:41,746
Now you know where to look.

